Yes. Think of this as your first day on a real project. Your lead says:

“Nikhil, here is the Git repo. Set it up on your local machine.”

You should know the flow from zero → repo available locally → ready to work.

Assume they give you:
https://github.com/company/payment-service.git

1. Check whether Git is installed
git --version 

If you get something like:
git version 2.5x.x

On Mac, if Git isn't installed, you can install the Apple command-line tools:
xcode-select --install
Or, if your company uses Homebrew:
brew install git
Then verify again:
git --version

2. Configure your Git identity

If they want your company identity globally:
git config --global user.name "Nikhil V"
git config --global user.email "nikhil@company.com"

Verify:
git config --global --list

3. Get access to the company's Git server

This is important. Installing Git doesn't automatically give you access.
Your company might use GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Azure DevOps, etc. Usually they'll give you either an HTTPS or SSH clone URL.

For SSH, first check:
ls -la ~/.ssh

Check for an existing key:
ls ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
If your company wants you to create one:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "nikhil@company.com"
Then:
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
You add that public key to the company's Git platform. Never share:
~/.ssh/id_ed25519

Create your local workspace

For example:

mkdir -p ~/projects
cd ~/projects

Now you're ready to bring the company repository down.

5. Clone the repository

Your lead might give you:

git@github.com:company/payment-service.git

Then:

git clone git@github.com:company/payment-service.git

Git creates:

projects/
└── payment-service/

Enter it:

cd payment-service
6. Immediately inspect the repo

First:

git status

You might see:

On branch main
Your branch is up to date with 'origin/main'.

nothing to commit, working tree clean

Then:

git remote -v

You should see something like:

origin  git@github.com:company/payment-service.git (fetch)
origin  git@github.com:company/payment-service.git (push)

This tells you:

LOCAL REPO
    ↕
origin
    ↕
COMPANY REMOTE REPO

Check branches:

git branch

Remote branches:

git branch -r

All branches:

git branch -a
7. Before starting work, sync

Suppose your team's main development branch is develop:

git switch develop
git pull

Then don't normally start making changes directly on develop.

Create your working branch:

git switch -c feature/payment-monitoring

Now:

git branch

might show:

develop
* feature/payment-monitoring

The * tells you which branch you're currently on.

8. Now you work

For example, your task might be changing:

Jenkinsfile
Dockerfile
helm/values.yaml
k8s/deployment.yaml

After making changes:

git status

See the difference:

git diff

Stage:

git add Jenkinsfile

Or all intended changes:

git add .

Commit:

git commit -m "Add SonarQube scan to CI pipeline"

Push your new branch:

git push -u origin feature/payment-monitoring

Then normally you create a Pull Request / Merge Request, someone reviews it, and it gets merged according to your team's process.

So keep this real-project flow in your head:

Install/check Git
        ↓
Configure identity
        ↓
Get repository access
        ↓
Clone company repo
        ↓
cd repo
        ↓
git status
git remote -v
git branch -a
        ↓
Switch to team's base branch
        ↓
git pull
        ↓
Create feature branch
        ↓
Make changes
        ↓
git status / git diff
        ↓
git add
        ↓
git commit
        ↓
git push
        ↓
Pull Request / Merge Request
        ↓
Review
        ↓
Merge
